From e695853c54f057c261234f162639a78102a1e0ed Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rishabh <iea@iitgn.ac.in>
Date: Tue, 22 Sep 2026 10:45:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Send to PostMan without writing a file

PostMan can open this app with the plant already named. When it has, the
dashboard offers one more button, and it posts back the SAME workbook
buildExcelBase64 already writes - all eight sheets, same headers, same
bytes. PostMan decodes it and runs its ordinary importer, so there is no
second format for either side to keep in step with.

Export and drop is untouched and stays the route that always works.

lib/postman-handoff.ts checks the origin PostMan hands us against a list of
PostMan's own deployments, and checks it again on every read. That origin
arrives in a URL and a URL can be written by anyone: without the list, any
site could open this app with origin=https://collector.example and be given
the plant's survey the moment the engineer pressed the button. Deliberately
a list and not a suffix match on .vercel.app, which every preview
deployment on Vercel would satisfy. NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTMAN_ORIGINS adds to it
for a preview build.

The query is remembered in sessionStorage because the parameters sit on the
landing URL and the first client-side navigation drops them; window.opener
survives that, so only the addressing would have been lost.

scripts/postman-bridge-check.ts writes a workbook from this app's real
export code, with a realistic plant - an incomer, a PCC, an MCC, two motors
and two APFC stages. Feeding it to PostMan is how the gap on the other side
was found: PostMan recognised two of the eight tabs and dropped the rest
without a word. That is fixed in rdudr/PostMAN (importFox, test/fox.mjs),
and the file this script writes is that test's fixture - regenerate it here
rather than hand-editing it there.

Pairs with rdudr/PostMAN docs/HANDOFF.md.

